"Places a defined uncertainty interval around an estimated curve across a continuous predictor or time axis.\n\nUse when: A fitted mean or other curve estimate needs uncertainty displayed across its domain. Bounds are available at a defensible set of predictor positions.\n\nAvoid when: Readers would mistake pointwise confidence for prediction of individual observations or simultaneous coverage.\n\nVisual grammar: The central line encodes the estimated curve. Lower and upper ribbon boundaries encode interval endpoints at each predictor value.\n\nBuild: Compute curve estimates and interval endpoints with a stated method and confidence level. Sort predictor positions and draw the ribbon behind the estimate. Name whether coverage is pointwise or simultaneous and retain gaps in unsupported regions.\n\nCheck: Check lower boundaries never exceed upper boundaries. Verify the same transformed scale is used for estimate and bounds. Confirm the band is not labeled as a prediction interval unless it predicts new observations.\n\nPitfalls: A 95% pointwise band does not imply 95% probability for an entire trajectory. Overlapping bands can obscure each other; prefer separate panels for multiple fitted groups.\n\nExample: Show a synthetic estimated demand curve with a pointwise 95% confidence ribbon and raw observations lightly visible.\n\nAlternatives: Error bar chart, Fan chart, Regression plot."
